WITH item AS (SELECT '2026-08-09-seguin-pork-ribs-peoples-choice'::text slug,'Viral'::text category,'Seguin Barbecue Joint Wins a Statewide Fan Vote for Texas’ Best Pork Ribs'::text title,'Schulze’s Pit Room beat 63 other smokehouses in a seven-week online bracket to claim the inaugural Texas Barbecue People’s Choice Award for pork ribs.'::text dek,'MySA'::text source_name,'https://www.mysanantonio.com/food/article/texas-best-pork-ribs-schulzes-pit-room-22354551.php'::text source_url,'2026-08-09T15:39:00Z'::timestamptz published_at,25::int score,FALSE::boolean is_breaking,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/seguin-pork-ribs.svg'::text image_url,'Editorial illustration of a Seguin barbecue pit, pork ribs and a Texas-shaped people’s-choice trophy.'::text image_alt_text,$body$A Seguin smokehouse has won a new kind of Texas barbecue bragging right. Schulze’s Pit Room was named the inaugural champion of the 2026 Texas Barbecue People’s Choice Award for Best Pork Ribs after seven weeks of fan voting, according to MySA. The competition started with 64 barbecue businesses and ended with Schulze’s defeating Mimsy’s Craft Barbecue & Steakhouse in the final round. Unlike a judges’ panel, the result reflects an online popularity contest, which gives the award a different but very Texas kind of significance.

Pork ribs often live in brisket’s shadow in Central Texas barbecue. The state’s most famous pit rooms are usually judged first by sliced beef, and tourists may order brisket automatically even when a restaurant’s strongest item is something else. A dedicated rib tournament gives pitmasters an excuse to make pork the headline. Schulze’s victory may also encourage travelers to look beyond the standard barbecue checklist and compare how different smokehouses handle texture, seasoning, smoke and glaze.

Seguin is well positioned to benefit from that attention. The city sits east of San Antonio along Interstate 10 and is close enough for a day trip from several major Central Texas population centers. A statewide food award can turn a restaurant into a destination, especially when social media gives voters a reason to post plates and campaign for their favorite. The competition itself becomes marketing for every restaurant in the bracket, not just the winner.

The People’s Choice format is important to understand. It does not establish an objective scientific ranking of ribs, and restaurants with larger or more engaged online followings can have an advantage. But that is partly the point. Fan-voted competitions measure enthusiasm and loyalty as much as technique. Schulze’s said the result felt especially meaningful because customers, rather than a small group of professional judges, selected the winner.

Ribs also expose a pitmaster’s technique in ways that are easy for diners to recognize. Meat that falls completely off the bone can actually be overcooked by competition standards, while tough ribs can be difficult to eat. The goal is often a bite that pulls cleanly without collapsing. Smoke, salt, pepper, sweetness and fat all have to work together across a rack. Small differences in temperature or holding time can change the result, which is why consistency matters when a restaurant becomes known for one item.

The award arrives during a period when beef prices have pushed some barbecue customers to look more seriously at pork and chicken. That does not mean ribs are cheap, but a restaurant capable of making pork a destination item has more flexibility when brisket costs rise. Texas barbecue has always evolved around livestock prices, available cuts and regional tastes. A renewed focus on pork can broaden menus without abandoning the central craft of wood-fired cooking.

There is also a broader tourism angle. Texas barbecue travel has become its own category, with visitors building road trips around famous pits. Awards, lists and online brackets help create routes that send customers into smaller cities instead of concentrating every trip in Austin or the largest metros. Seguin already has historic attractions and easy highway access; a rib championship adds another reason for travelers to stop rather than pass through.

For Schulze’s, the challenge is what happens after the announcement. A surge of first-time customers can strain parking, kitchens and inventory. The worst outcome for an award-winning restaurant is for new visitors to arrive after a long drive only to find the signature item sold out or inconsistent. Managing the attention becomes part of protecting the reputation that won the vote in the first place.

For everyone else, the tournament gives Texans another food argument that cannot really be settled. A fan vote says Schulze’s supporters showed up and carried their restaurant through the bracket. It does not stop anyone from nominating a different rack of ribs at the next family gathering. That endless debate is one reason barbecue rankings work so well online: every winner creates both congratulations and a new list of places people insist should have won instead.

The result also underscores how much restaurant discovery has shifted from traditional critics to communities of customers. A generation ago, a small-town barbecue joint might have depended on newspaper reviews and word of mouth. Today a bracket, a short video or a photograph of a rack of ribs can introduce the business to people hundreds of miles away in a day. That reach can be powerful, but it also means the public reputation of a restaurant is constantly being formed by ordinary diners. Winning a fan vote is valuable precisely because it shows that enough customers felt strongly enough to spend time voting and sharing the contest.

This article is an independent Keep TX Red rewrite based on MySA reporting published July 22, 2026. MySA reported that Schulze’s Pit Room defeated a field of 64 smokehouses after seven weeks of voting to win the inaugural 2026 Texas Barbecue People’s Choice Award for Best Pork Ribs.$body$::text body), prepared AS (SELECT *,jsonb_build_object('updated','2026-08-09','intro',jsonb_build_array(split_part(body,E'\n\n',1)),'sections',jsonb_build_array(jsonb_build_object('heading','The story','paragraphs',jsonb_build_array(body))),'faq',jsonb_build_array(),'sources',jsonb_build_array(jsonb_build_object('label',source_name||' — original report','url',source_url)),'keyTakeaways',jsonb_build_array(dek)) body_json FROM item) INSERT INTO public.daily_articles(slug,internal_url,is_ingested,category,title,dek,source_name,source_url,published_at,score,is_breaking,kind,body,body_json,featured_image_url,image_url,image_alt_text) SELECT slug,'/news/'||slug,FALSE,category,title,dek,source_name,source_url,published_at,score,is_breaking,'news',body,body_json,image_url,image_url,image_alt_text FROM prepared ON CONFLICT(slug) DO UPDATE SET category=EXCLUDED.category,title=EXCLUDED.title,dek=EXCLUDED.dek,source_name=EXCLUDED.source_name,source_url=EXCLUDED.source_url,published_at=EXCLUDED.published_at,score=EXCLUDED.score,is_breaking=EXCLUDED.is_breaking,body=EXCLUDED.body,body_json=EXCLUDED.body_json,featured_image_url=EXCLUDED.featured_image_url,image_url=EXCLUDED.image_url,image_alt_text=EXCLUDED.image_alt_text;